* Popular Machine Learning Models
** Supervised Learning
*** Definition: Models trained on labeled data to predict outputs; focuses on mapping inputs to known targets.
*** Linear Regression
**** Tasks: Predicting continuous values (e.g., house prices, stock values).
**** Why Chosen: Simple, interpretable, assumes linear relationships; fast training, low computational cost.
**** Details: Uses least squares minimization; prone to underfitting if data is non-linear; extensions include Ridge/Lasso for regularization.
*** Logistic Regression
**** Tasks: Binary or multi-class classification (e.g., spam detection, disease diagnosis).
**** Why Chosen: Probabilistic outputs via sigmoid/softmax; interpretable coefficients; handles linear separability well.
**** Details: Extension of linear regression for classification; uses log-loss; regularized variants prevent overfitting.
*** Decision Trees
**** Tasks: Classification and regression (e.g., customer segmentation, risk assessment).
**** Why Chosen: Highly interpretable (tree structure); handles non-linear data and mixed types; no normalization needed.
**** Details: Splits data based on feature thresholds; prone to overfitting; use pruning or ensembles like Random Forests.
*** Random Forests
**** Tasks: Classification/regression in complex datasets (e.g., fraud detection, image classification).
**** Why Chosen: Ensemble of decision trees reduces overfitting; high accuracy; feature importance ranking.
**** Details: Bagging with random feature subsets; robust to noise; slower than single trees but parallelizable.
*** Support Vector Machines (SVM)
**** Tasks: Classification, regression (e.g., text categorization, bioinformatics).
**** Why Chosen: Effective in high-dimensional spaces; maximizes margin for better generalization; kernel trick for non-linearity.
**** Details: Uses support vectors; kernels like RBF for complex boundaries; computationally intensive for large data.
*** K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
**** Tasks: Classification/regression (e.g., recommendation systems, anomaly detection).
**** Why Chosen: Instance-based (lazy learning); simple, no training phase; effective for local patterns.
**** Details: Distance-based (e.g., Euclidean); sensitive to scale and outliers; choose K via cross-validation.
*** Naive Bayes
**** Tasks: Classification (e.g., sentiment analysis, document classification).
**** Why Chosen: Fast, probabilistic; assumes feature independence; performs well on text data.
**** Details: Based on Bayes' theorem; variants like Gaussian/Multinomial; robust to irrelevant features.
*** Neural Networks (Feedforward)
**** Tasks: Complex pattern recognition (e.g., image/speech processing).
**** Why Chosen: Universal approximators; learn hierarchical features; scalable with data/hardware.
**** Details: Layers of neurons with activations (e.g., ReLU); trained via backpropagation; risk of overfitting without regularization.
**** Variants: CNNs for grids (images/sequences); RNNs/LSTMs for sequences (time series/NLP).
** Unsupervised Learning
*** Definition: Models identify patterns in unlabeled data; useful for exploration and preprocessing.
*** K-Means Clustering
**** Tasks: Grouping similar data (e.g., market segmentation, image compression).
**** Why Chosen: Simple, scalable; partitions data into K clusters based on centroids.
**** Details: Iterative assignment/update; sensitive to initialization (use K-Means++); choose K via elbow/silhouette methods.
*** Hierarchical Clustering
**** Tasks: Building dendrograms for nested groups (e.g., taxonomy, gene expression).
**** Why Chosen: No need to specify clusters upfront; visualizes hierarchy.
**** Details: Agglomerative (bottom-up) or divisive; linkage methods (e.g., ward); computationally expensive for large N.
*** Principal Component Analysis (PCA)
**** Tasks: Dimensionality reduction (e.g., feature extraction, visualization).
**** Why Chosen: Linear technique; maximizes variance; reduces multicollinearity.
**** Details: Eigen decomposition of covariance; select components by explained variance; assumes linearity.
*** Autoencoders
**** Tasks: Anomaly detection, denoising, feature learning (e.g., image reconstruction).
**** Why Chosen: Neural network-based; learns compressed representations; unsupervised deep learning.
**** Details: Encoder-decoder structure; variants like variational for generative tasks.
*** Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)
**** Tasks: Data generation (e.g., synthetic images, style transfer).
**** Why Chosen: Produces realistic samples; adversarial training improves quality.
**** Details: Generator vs. Discriminator; unstable training; extensions like DCGAN for stability.
** Semi-Supervised Learning
*** Definition: Combines limited labeled data with abundant unlabeled; leverages patterns to improve efficiency.
*** Self-Training
**** Tasks: When labels are scarce (e.g., web page classification).
**** Why Chosen: Iterative pseudo-labeling; boosts performance with unlabeled data.
**** Details: Train on labeled, predict on unlabeled, add confident predictions; risk of error propagation.
*** Graph-Based Methods
**** Tasks: Label propagation (e.g., social networks).
**** Why Chosen: Uses data similarity graphs; smooths labels across connections.
**** Details: Algorithms like Label Spreading; effective for structured data.
** Reinforcement Learning
*** Definition: Agent learns via trial-and-error interactions with environment; optimizes rewards.
*** Q-Learning
**** Tasks: Discrete action spaces (e.g., game playing, robotics pathfinding).
**** Why Chosen: Off-policy; tabular or with function approximation; model-free.
**** Details: Updates Q-values based on Bellman equation; exploration via epsilon-greedy.
*** Deep Q-Networks (DQN)
**** Tasks: High-dimensional states (e.g., Atari games, autonomous driving).
**** Why Chosen: Combines Q-Learning with deep nets; handles image inputs.
**** Details: Experience replay, target networks for stability; variants like Double DQN.
*** Policy Gradient Methods (e.g., REINFORCE, PPO)
**** Tasks: Continuous actions (e.g., robot control, portfolio optimization).
**** Why Chosen: Directly optimizes policy; handles stochastic environments.
**** Details: Gradient ascent on expected rewards; PPO clips for stable updates.
** Ensemble Methods
*** Definition: Combine multiple models for improved performance; reduces variance/bias.
*** Boosting (e.g., XGBoost, AdaBoost)
**** Tasks: Tabular data prediction (e.g., competitions like Kaggle).
**** Why Chosen: Sequential error correction; high accuracy; handles missing data.
**** Details: XGBoost: Gradient boosting with regularization; fast, scalable.
*** Bagging (e.g., Random Forests)
**** Tasks: As above; stabilizes unstable learners.
**** Why Chosen: Parallel training; reduces variance.
**** Details: Bootstrap sampling; out-of-bag evaluation.
** Key Considerations Across Models
*** Data Requirements: Supervised needs labels; unsupervised explores structure.
*** Evaluation: Supervised (accuracy, F1); Unsupervised (silhouette, inertia); RL (cumulative reward).
*** Trade-offs: Interpretability vs. Accuracy (trees simple, NNs black-box); Compute (simple models fast, deep learning resource-intensive).
*** Trends: Transfer learning, hybrid models (e.g., CNN + RNN); ethical aspects (bias mitigation).
